Run a NeXT with a 68060 in place of the 68040 (nCpuLevel = 5)

NeXT never made one; this is enough of a 68060 for ROM 3.3 (the Turbo
machines') and Linux.  The CPU core is WinUAE's 68060 MMU core (33),
which Previous didn't generate:

- gencpu generates core 33, built and listed for the 68060; its cycle
  counts are half the 68040's (about twice the work per clock, as
  Motorola's figures have it), DBcc kept at the 68040's for the ROM's
  delay loops.  Its run loop runs the DSP, i860 and events through
  run_other_MPUs() as the 68040's does.
- The ROM keeps data in MSP and ISP from its first instructions, which
  the 68060 dropped: MOVEC still reaches them.
- The ROM's FPU test uses FScc, which a 68060 leaves to software: the
  FPU executes everything the 68040's did (fpu_no_unimplemented off).
  Integer instructions the 68060 lacks (64-bit MUL/DIV, MOVEP, CAS2,
  CHK2...) trap as on the real thing; Linux's 060 package emulates them.
- The ROM's bus error handler, which pops the frame itself to recover
  from its probes, has no case for the 68060's access fault frame
  (format 4): rom.c adds one in unused ROM space and corrects the ROM's
  CRC.  Not found in ROM 2.5, so only the Turbo machines boot.
- The PCR reports revision 6, and the debugger reads memory through the
  68040/060 MMU functions.

